    {
      "id": "ideas",
      "type": "explain",
      "title": "Classes and objects",
      "points": [
        "A **class** is a blueprint; `new ClassName()` builds an **object** from it.",
        "**Fields** hold each object’s own data. **Methods** act on that object’s data (`this`).",
        "A **constructor** runs when the object is created. Use it to set the starting values of the fields.",
        "Keep fields `private` and change them only through methods. That is **encapsulation**."
      ],
      "pattern": "class Student {\n    private String name;\n    Student(String name) { this.name = name; }\n    String getName() { return name; }\n}"
    },
    {
      "id": "p2",
      "type": "predict",
      "prompt": "A classic trap. What does this print?",
      "code": "class Counter {\n    private int count;\n    void tick() { count++; }\n    int get() { return count; }\n}\n\npublic class Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Counter a = new Counter();\n        Counter b = a;\n        b.tick();\n        System.out.println(a.get());\n    }\n}",
      "options": [
        {
          "text": "It doesn't compile",
          "why": "Assigning one object variable to another is allowed; it copies the reference."
        },
        {
          "text": "2",
          "why": "tick() ran once, on the one shared object."
        },
        {
          "text": "1",
          "why": "Yes. a and b refer to one and the same object."
        },
        {
          "text": "0",
          "why": "b = a doesn’t copy the object. Both names now point at the SAME counter, so ticking b ticks a."
        }
      ],
      "answer": 2
    },
    {
      "id": "fill",
      "type": "fill",
      "prompt": "Your turn. Complete the constructor so a new account starts with the opening balance.",
      "code": "class Account {\n    private int balance;\n\n    Account(int opening) {\n        ____;\n    }\n\n    int getBalance() {\n        return balance;\n    }\n}\n\npublic class Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        System.out.println(new Account(500).getBalance());\n        System.out.println(new Account(20).getBalance());\n    }\n}",
      "accepted": [
        "balance = opening",
        "this.balance = opening"
      ],
      "wrong": [
        "opening = balance",
        "int balance = opening",
        "balance = 500"
      ],
      "hint": "Store the parameter (opening) into the field (balance).",
      "explain": "`this.balance = opening` copies the value passed to `new` into the object’s field. Writing `int balance = opening` would create a separate local variable and leave the field at 0.",
      "expectedOutput": "500\n20"
    }
  ]
